<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hernando de Soto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–<a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hernando de Soto was a Spanish conquistador who took part in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and in 1539 led a large expedition into the south-eastern interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Over four years his army marched through the Mississippian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, wintering at the Apalachee capital, and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>, where he died in 1542.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto.jpg" alt="Hernando de Soto" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Sartain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Soto_by_Telfer_%26_Sartain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hernando de Soto was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, in what is now the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>, to parents who were both hidalgos, nobles of modest means. Badajoz, Barcarrota and Jerez de los Caballeros each claim to be his birthplace; Ursula Lamb considers Jerez most likely, and his will asked that his body be buried there. The region was poor, and young men sought fortune overseas. He sailed to the New World with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedrarias-davila">Pedro Arias Dávila</a>, the first governor of Panama, landing as a teenager in 1514.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>De Soto took part in an expedition to Veragua in 1520 and in the conquest of Nicaragua from 1524, where he received an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> and an office in León. He became known as a horseman and tactician, and for extorting native villages by seizing their chiefs. He then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and became one of his captains. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> he led one of three mounted groups in the attack on the Inca Atahualpa. During the king's captivity in 1533 he grew friendly with him and taught him chess. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1536 with plunder from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a> and married Isabel de Bobadilla.</p><p>The crown granted him the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the right to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. With about 620 Spanish and Portuguese men, among them Atlantic Creoles who were enslaved, and with horses and pigs, he landed on the Florida coast in May 1539. He found Juan Ortiz, a Spaniard living among the Mocoso, who served as interpreter in Timucuan-speaking areas. The expedition wintered at the Apalachee village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anhaica">Anhaica</a>, then went north-east through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and was received by the female chief of Cofitachequi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, where it found no gold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The army crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a> into eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> on 8 May 1541, building flatboats and crossing at night. It then travelled west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. De Soto died of a fever on 21 May 1542 in the native village of Guachoya. He had persuaded local people that he was an immortal Son of the Sun, so his men hid his death, and by one account sank his body in the Mississippi. Luis de Moscoso Alvarado took command, and the survivors built boats and descended the river. The expedition had lost nearly half its men and found neither treasure nor a site for a colony.</p><p>The Spanish counted it a failure. Its pigs escaped and became ancestors of feral razorbacks, and clashes it provoked set many peoples against Europeans. Whether it brought epidemics is disputed, since the first documented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic in the south-east dates from 1696. The route is debated beyond Mabila in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, where John R. Swanton and Charles M. Hudson differ.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hernando de Soto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The chroniclers of <b>Hernando de Soto</b>’s expedition describe chiefs borne in litters by their leading men, a woman ruler at Cofitachequi in present-day South Carolina, and the paramount chiefdom of Coosa in north-western Georgia, whose ruler commanded a string of chiefdoms from eastern Tennessee into Alabama.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Paramount chiefdoms centred on Moundville and Etowah rose and fell across the South-East, and <b>Hernando de Soto</b>’s expedition marched through Cofitachequi and Coosa in 1540.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hernando de Soto</b> argues that state protection of property rights, with ownership clearly recorded, turns physical assets into capital.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>When Pánfilo de Narváez arrived in 1528 and <b>Hernando de Soto</b> wintered at their capital, Anhaica, in 1539 to 1540, the Apalachee were populous and warlike.</blockquote><a href="/state/apalachee-chiefdom">Apalachee chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chickasaw descended from Mississippian chiefdoms of northern Mississippi; the Spanish of <b>Hernando de Soto's</b> expedition met them, as Chicaza, in 1540 to 1541, and were attacked in their winter camp.</blockquote><a href="/state/chickasaw-nation">Chickasaw Nation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1540 <b>Hernando de Soto's</b> army was met by a woman ruler, known to the Spanish as the Lady of Cofitachequi, who presented strings of pearls; the Spanish looted pearls from a temple-burial house and took her hostage, though she escaped.</blockquote><a href="/state/cofitachequi">Cofitachequi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerez de los Caballeros, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Guachoya. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.32,-6.77&amp;year=1527" aria-label="Jerez de los Caballeros on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4382 787 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hernando de Soto"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4832 1057h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4832 1057h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.32,-6.77&amp;year=1527">What was here in 1527? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
